NTERVIEWER] And how old were you on September 1st, 1939? [INTERVIEWEE] I was 13, not even 14 years old. [INTERVIEWER] You described part of that first day to me. Once the bomb dropped on your building, what did your family do? [INTERVIEWEE] We were, we went to our grandmother's to live, my grandmother had a big apartment. So we all went, in the same town, and we all went to this uh, to this apartment. We were staying over there. [INTERVIEWER] So her apartment building had not been bombed then. [INTERVIEWEE] No. No. No. [INTERVIEWER] What changes took place from that time on? What were the first changes that took place after the bombing? [INTERVIEWEE] We were separated from everything and we were starting on to get rations of food, very little. And uh, my my father was a religious man. We had to take off right away, they took off the beard and and um... [INTERVIEWER] Describe this to me. What happened? Did you witness their removing your father's beard? [INTERVIEWEE] Uh yes, I was always with my father and always... [INTERVIEWER] What happened? [INTERVIEWEE] It was a very big shock to our to us. We were not prepared to all of a sudden such a change in our lives for everything. And uh... [INTERVIEWER] You say they removed your father's beard. Did they come to your home? [INTERVIEWEE] No, it was orders. It was orders that everybody has to take off the beard. And we had to wear the the yellow stripes and that we should know that we are Judas. you know, so it was uh in the back or on the arm, we always, and it was uh, we were not anymore human beings for a number. [INTERVIEWER] As a 13-year-old child, what were your feelings when you had to put this armband on? [INTERVIEWEE] You know when you are when you are in your life, when you grow up to learn to obey, you have to obey. You cannot do it any other way. That's the way we have to do if you want to survive. [INTERVIEWER] What happened to your father's store? [INTERVIEWEE] To the store? It was right away bombed the first