ery little to survive. Just with very little. [INTERVIEWER] You mentioned that amongst the older people, sometimes they took ill. What happened if there was a death within the ghetto during this time? Were you allowed to bury your dead? [INTERVIEWEE] They they were taking them out, not a funeral, but they were taking them out some place to be buried. [INTERVIEWER] What were some of the scenes that you remember on the streets that you saw during '39 to '41 within the Brzeziny ghetto? [INTERVIEWEE] You see, I was um so uh, I personally was so down that I didn't go out more than I worked. and I just kept to myself and uh didn't mingle with nobody. [INTERVIEWER] What about your brothers? [INTERVIEWEE] They were young children and they were we just stay together. There were not nothing to talk about. [INTERVIEWER] Were they working? [INTERVIEWEE] No, my brother was very young. my children, my brother was very young. [INTERVIEWER] When they came to take the children in '41, what happened? [INTERVIEWEE] they were they were 10 years old already. They didn't take them then. They didn't take them then. [INTERVIEWER] Now this incident with the children occurred in 1941. What else happened in 1941? [INTERVIEWEE] It's always, there always was selections. They always took out people what they didn't like or what, that's the reason I we wouldn't walk out much in the street because we didn't know who will be next to be picked up because they had they had to take so many people away. So they always have to be people. The people, whom they picked up in the street or he was working on the street or something. And so my parents uh wanted us more to be home that we should not be the one, the next to go. you know. [INTERVIEWER] Did they ever come to take your parents or your great-grandparents? [INTERVIEWEE] Not from this ghetto. Not from this ghetto. [INTERVIEWER] Did you remember anyone trying to escape from the Brzeziny ghetto? [INTERVIEWEE] Uh, a lot of people went in hiding and they never made it because the Polacks did report them. [INTERVIEWER] And what happened when they were found? [INTERVIEWEE] They were killed. They were just killed. [INTERVIEWER] Was the whole community called to witness this once it happened? [INTERVIEWEE] No. No. [INTERVIEWER] Were there any other punishments that you witnessed? [INTERVIEWEE] Uh, I I cannot recall. It's such a long time ago and I tried always to put it out of my head, not to remember everything. [INTERVIEWER] What about you? Were you ever the recipient of any punishment from the Germans? [INTERVIEWEE] Not in this ghetto. Not in this ghetto. No. [INTERVIEWER] How long did you remain in this ghetto? [INTERVIEWEE] Till 1942 in I think it must have been April. [INTERVIEWER] It was clean, the ghetto was cleaned out. During the winter months, when you were in this ghetto, were you able to get enough fuel to keep warm? [INTERVIEWEE] We didn't get no fuel. We didn't get nothing. [INTERVIEWER] How did you manage during those very cold winter months? [INTERVIEWEE] Ah, just we we covered ourselves with what we had. and we were laying onto the blankets what we had. And that's all. [INTERVIEWER] What happened in April 1942? [INTERVIEWEE] Then they took us um out and they took us into everybody what could walk, and it was a 22-mile walk to the Łódź ghetto. And this ghetto was liquidated. So um, who in your family went on this march? [INTERVIEWEE] uh my whole family went on this march. [INTERVIEWER] Including your great-grandparents? [INTERVIEWEE] No, they were dead already then. [INTERVIEWER] What happened? When did they die? [INTERVIEWEE] I right in the beginning of the of the war. must have been in 1940. [INTERVIEWER] From illness? [INTERVIEWEE] I think more for pain and from uh and see what was going on when they saw it when they were hanging the people and they saw that this and they didn't have enough to eat, none of them. and they were just laying in bed and they were old and they died. [INTERVIEWER] And what about your grandparents? [INTERVIEWEE] My grandparents were young. and when my grandfather came in in the Łódź ghetto, he... [INTERVIEWER] Did they go on this 22-mile walk with you? [INTERVIEWEE] Yes, yes, yes. [INTERVIEW's a very, very painful march to go. What was the weather like? It was April, but was it still cold or? [INTERVIEWEE] It was cold. Just we and we we marched, we marched, I don't remember how long we did march. We we came over to a place to I think it was Czarnieckiego, it was a like a meeting place. And over there they also selected people right away to die and to send them away to, later on we found out they sent them away to Auschwitz. We didn't know that. [INTERVIEWER] Did they do that while you were marching or in this... [INTERVIEWEE] No, no, no, this was a lot of them what a lot of people what were old, they were couldn't make it. So we didn't we couldn't turn around our heads even to see what was going on. [INTERVIEWER] What about food and water during this march? [INTERVIEWEE] we just got a little water and maybe we got a piece of bread, maybe, I don't remember. [INTERVIEWER] And what about the nights? Where did you spend the nights? [INTERVIEWEE] So, when we were walking, we didn't spend there. We were just laying down and and walked again. Just um, when we came to to Łódź, then they make also selections where they took away people what they didn't like. [INTERVIEWER] How long did this 22-mile march take? [INTERVIEWEE] I really cannot remember. [INTERVIEWER] What was the condition of your parents and grandparents during this time? [INTERVIEWEE] We were holding on to one another with a little water, with a and just to survive, we hope that we're going, we're going into Łódź. big city. So we came in, we were thinking that we're going to go, we're going to make a life there, we're going to we're going to... to work, and it was not that way. It was the first time my grandfather came into Łódź. He we had to sign a paper that the the number. and there was um the there was a bridge in the ghetto where you had to go over to my grandfather went over yet then. and uh, on the way back, he didn't feel good. they already put him in a little wagon. that what we heard because we were on the other side of the ghetto. and uh, and he died right away the next day. [INTERVIEWER] Was he with you at the time of his death or [INTERVIEWEE] No, he was with his wife, with my grandmother. The next day he died. He must have been very young because my father was in the ghetto maybe 40 40, 1942, he was 44 years old, and my grandfather maybe was 64 years old. [INTERVIEWER] When you first entered Łódź into the ghetto, what did you see? [INTERVIEWEE] We we went to we were also everybody just as I explained to you, everybody had to give you a room where to live. So, uh, this room where we got was where my mother's sister lived in that ghetto. And she had also three rooms. So, uh, uh in one room was living already a cousin, she came before the ghetto was closed, they lived in Łódź, they a cousin and with two with two grown children and my aunt was living with her daughter and son. And um, in the other room and we lived in the kitchen. and with our family. So we were this three rooms we had, we were three families. and then what I saw right when I came in, my cousin, my first cousin must have been was a year older than me. and he was working in the ghetto, carrying very heavy work, racks or what in this ghetto, what he was working. And I saw the way he came home and he was spitting out blood. and he was and he couldn't, the next day, this was right in the beginning when I came in, he must have been not even 18 years old. And he was so bleeding from from the mouth. and I just couldn't believe it that what is this? and it took a few days and he died. [INTERVIEWER] What were some of the scenes that you saw on the streets on Łódź at this time? [INTERVIEWEE] You see, in the in the streets, you only saw people going to work because we had to buy this rations to be able to to to to survive. We didn't had no money. and when you were getting once a week a bread for the week, so you needed something, so they put you to work. You didn't, you didn't associate with people to come into the house, no. [INTERVIEWER] No, I wasn't asking in that sense. I meant what were some of the things that you saw happening to people on the streets? [INTERVIEWEE] People were going in the street and falling in the street for hunger. And that was the hardest thing to see. Young and old. [INTERVIEWER] And what did they do with, there were bodies on the street. How did they dispose of the bodies once they... [INTERVIEWEE] They came, they came, uh, something like a wagon, wagon because it was in in this side was um, uh Stacheldraht, that's like a gate. and in the other side in in the middle the the subway was going like a trolley car was going in in Łódź. and we were not allowed to go across the street. We had to go through the bridge to go across the street. So the, there was some doors open where for them to take out these people, not on the trolley car, just with some wagon and they took them away. But nobody knows where they were buried or something. It was not a funeral or I don't know where my grandfather was buried. We couldn't go to the funeral or something. I don't know nothing about it. And my grandmother, same day, same thing happened with my grandmother. My grandfather died, my grandmother went came to live with us. From drinking this, there was coffee, not the regular coffee, just the what what as left something and this what she was drinking, so she became ill and her stomach was so big. I don't know how you call that coffee, what it's just like not the leftovers, not the regular coffee, it's a name for that in Poland. in Polish you say, cykoria. So this was in this she was drinking and then my grandmother had such a big stomach from drinking that most of the people that were drinking that that coffee, they they became very ill and they got very big stomachs. and and they she couldn't survive. She just died very right away too. [INTERVIEWER] What was the method of distributing food to the people in the ghetto? [INTERVIEWEE] There were stores. and in the stores you had to stay in line and to be able to to have the rations, if you have uh uh some the money to buy for that money, so you only bought what you had the ration for the week. [INTERVIEWER] And what was the ration for the week? [INTERVIEWEE] Very little, very little. It was um maybe a bread for the week, a small bread. [INTERVIEWER] For each of you or for the family? [INTERVIEWEE] I think for the family and that we had to divide a piece of bread and my brother was sick, my younger brother, he had water in the knee. So my mother took away, she they took away from us the piece of bread. She was thinking she will save that that he will survive when he will have the piece of bread. [INTERVIEWER] Describe the hunger to me. [INTERVIEWEE] I must have weighed about 75 pounds. And it was, we learned to live with hunger. We were eating leaves, where we were if we could get the leaves from certain so we were trying to cook the leaves or the not with with beets or something, just the leaves from anything. we were to be able to just to to have something in the stomach. We couldn't sleep for hunger. It was very, very hard. [INTERVIEWER] Was there any medical care in the ghetto? [INTERVIEWEE] No. Not what I can remember that Just, I I remember that my brother used to I the the smallest one, this he must have been he was 13 when he was sent to Auschwitz. So he was 11. So he was staying in the street selling saccharin to be able to you could be able to to to get to to buy the rations. [INTERVIEWER] Where was he able to get the saccharin? [INTERVIEWEE] I, somebody must have brought it in or something that he uh, that he that he that that he wanted he was he wanted to live. and he wanted to survive. He had to buy this to get this ration out. So uh, we had a Führer in uh in Łódź ghetto, his name was Rumkowski. And he passed by and he saw with his horse and wagon and he saw my brother selling the saccharin so he took him away. I just, my brother, my little brother was, if he would have lived, he would be brilliant guy. So he must have told him something. And this guy that Rumkowski could not sleep. And around 3:00 in the morning, he sent his police out to that Czarnieckiego where he's supposed to be send away to Auschwitz at that time. and he said, "Let him go home, he's so brilliant." And at 3:00 in the morning, the police brought him back. He was this was just a miracle. Just somehow he didn't survive. [INTERVIEWER] You referred to him, Rumkowski, as we have a Führer. [INTERVIEWEE] Yeah. [INTERVIEWER] Tell me about that. [INTERVIEWEE] I did not have much, he was just like he was filling out orders. He had to do certain things somehow somebody had to do it, so he was the one what is doing it. So not everybody wants to be a kapo, not everybody wants to be a somebody, and certain people for a piece of bread, they will do anything. So he was [INTERVIEWER] What were some of the other things that you were witness to Chaim Rumkowski doing? [INTERVIEWEE] I was, I when I came to the ghetto, to the Łódź ghetto, they signed me up to work, so I didn't have no much business with him or with anyone. I was working in a straw factory where we were making, most of this people what came in from Brzeziny, they put us to work in that straw where you have to make braids for the shoes, for the soldiers, for the German soldiers shoes. That was wet straw to make braids into shoe soles, but we had to do that. [INTERVIEWER] How many hours a day did you work in this factory? [INTERVIEWEE] Oh many, many hours. Not the factory, it was like a open thing because that straw was laying and we had to braid it at a very and my mother was sick already and my mother was sick, so I had to braid it uh very fast to make it for my mother the the portions too that. [INTERVIEWER] So you were doing your mother's work as well as your work? [INTERVIEWEE] Yes, so I made her let her sit down a little bit. [INTERVIEWER] Were you fed during the hours that you were working? [INTERVIEWEE] No. No. Nothing. Just sometimes we were getting a soup sometimes. [INTERVIEWER] Were you aware of any underground activity while you were in the ghetto? [INTERVIEWEE] No, just where we were working in the in the in the in the in the straw resort, there were rooms. In that rooms, they were full of blood like on the walls and then the people were telling us that when they bring back the clothes, we couldn't understand that, bring back clothes and the clothes is so with blood that that I don't know what they did with it and then and and then they were giving some clothes to people what they they they send it, must have been sent it back from some place and there was all bloody clothes and bloody walls on this where we were working in this room. [INTERVIEWER] So you didn't hear of any underground. Did you witness or hear about any acts of resistance in the ghetto? [INTERVIEWEE] No. We didn't know nothing what was going on. There was no radios, there was no papers. [INTERVIEWER] No, I meant right in your area. Did you ever see anybody try to resist the German officers when they were ordered or the German soldiers when they were ordered to do something? [INTERVIEWEE] No. [INTERVIEWER] Did you ever witness any punishment in the ghetto? [INTERVIEWEE] I personally didn't see this because I was just working and trying to make the days go by to and then to walk home. It was a big walk. It was maybe an hour walk from one, I was the other part of the city. [INTERVIEWER] You mentioned that your mother was ill. Was she able to do this? [INTERVIEWEE] We I, we had somebody on the other side on the bridge where she remained uh, with neighbors and she remained over there sleeping or be there. [INTERVIEWER] And you did her work? [INTERVIEWEE] And I did her work. [INTERVIEWER] What about your father during these years? [INTERVIEWEE] My father had a tough time to get to work in the ghetto. It was very, very hard. That's the reason uh it was so hard that my brother had to to get to get that few what was it Marks or the this money to buy the rations because he was a watchman, I think, for a while and some garden or something but he did not, they did not give him work. [INTERVIEWER] And what about your other brother? [INTERVIEWEE] My other brother, I don't recall what he was doing. He was 13 years old. I don't remember what he was doing. [INTERVIEWER] Were you aware of any religious observances in the ghetto during this time? [INTERVIEWEE] You see, the one what um were what were religious at home and the older the people like my parents, they still would hold on to to the and pray every day. and and a lot like I was very religious and in the ghetto, I just didn't do it because I had so much anger in me and so much to live through the day and to be able not to sleep at night because for hunger, but I didn't I didn't pray anymore. [INTERVIEWER] If there was a specific holiday, did you within the walls of your own apartment try to observe that holiday? [INTERVIEWEE] Yes, we did try. We did try. [INTERVIEWER] How did you, which holidays and how were you able to observe them? [INTERVIEWEE] We just, uh, when it was Yom Kippur, it was not a hard day to fast because we were fasting all the time. and we didn't have nothing. Just we made it, we put a tablecloth on, we put the and we had maybe a piece of candle on, so we put and we made it if we were getting some flour, so we were making a piece of a challah or something, just to make it look that it would be that we know we know every holiday what it was. And uh, on Passover, it was such a nice holiday, just we did not eat, if we did not get bread, we did not, my father would not let us eat even the piece of bread. We did not eat. We put it away for after Passover. [INTERVIEWER] During the week of Passover then, what could you eat? What did you have to eat? [INTERVIEWEE] I was trying to eat or we were eating we were this we had somebody there what what had a little garden and he was selling, so he he he ate the potatoes and he sold us the the the the peels from the potatoes or the or the the leaves from the beets or the leaves from something else and that was our food to be able to survive. And we with with with very little cooking, so we try to we never know to eat peels of potatoes or something, just we had to do it. [INTERVIEWER] During your first years in the Łódź ghetto, were you aware of any suicide attempts that were made? [INTERVIEWEE] We know what was going on. Just, you know, every day was something else, that somebody died. and just I personally, from my family, they were sent away or they died with natural death, with from hunger. and the rest, we all went to Auschwitz at 1944. [INTERVIEWER] Now, prior to that, um, your mother, you mentioned, was ill. How was she able to continue on? [INTERVIEWEE] No, she wasn't, she was a young woman. She was just ill from pain to see what her children go through and and sometimes she had temperature. Just she made it. She she fought to survive to help the children. [INTERVIEWER] Did you know of anyone who attempted to escape from this part of the Łódź ghetto? [INTERVIEWEE] I my friends personally, they did not escape. We were not aggressive people to to, we were afraid. We were thinking tomorrow the war will be over and we're going to make it. And uh, did not, so I didn't get in touch with these people because they didn't have to, we died slowly. Every day somebody else died. [INTERVIEWER] But were you hearing about it? [INTERVIEWEE] Of course we heard that people commit suicide, that people run away. A lot of people made it because for some people what went away for and they they went out from the ghetto and through some place and they just were going places or they they were just running, hiding in the woods and till and sometimes the Polacks would give them up or they kill them. And some made it, very few, just they made it. [INTERVIEWER] Did you ever witness any, the punishment of anyone who had attempted to escape from the ghetto? [INTERVIEWEE] No, I personally did not witness this. [INTERVIEWER] How long did you remain in this Łódź ghetto? [INTERVIEWEE] Uh, till 1944, August 18. [INTERVIEWER] And what happened on August 18th? [INTERVIEWEE] and during the night, around 2:00 in the morning, and the Jewish police came in and it was my father's brother still with us, one brother and uh and they came for me, they came for him. and for me. So [INTERVIEWER] For your father and for you or for your father's brother? [INTERVIEWEE] No, no, no, my father's brother. He must have been about 22 years old. And we went, I went along with him. and [INTERVIEWER] Describe that incident to me if you can. [INTERVIEWEE] It was the most it was the most awful thing in my life. I was going out from the house. My mother and my father was crying. and then my little brother what I, I will not forget. He take, "Take me with me, take, I should take him." I said, "They don't ask for you, they ask for me. I cannot take you." So he said, because it was so bad in that ghetto, he wanted to go with me. I didn't know where I was going. So, uh, we went away and we cried and then they took us to Czarnieckiego to and from there they took us to Auschwitz with a train. And I was in that train a whole night crying and crying and crying because I left my parents with my two brothers. And it was so bad. It was the worst nightmare in my life to leave them. and I came into Auschwitz. [INTERVIEWER] Let us go back for a moment though, if we can. Um, how long were you on this train? [INTERVIEWEE] I think we went by 24 hours on that train. [INTERVIEWER] Describe the train. [INTERVIEWEE] We were just in cattle like when you put in horses, they have it much better. We were in and just there wasn't an inch where you could move. Not to lay down. Just not even to move. We were staying up a whole night and a whole day. And they only give you one pail. And there were not windows because when the horses, it's like horses, there were just little windows. So during the night, we were throwing out from that pail with people. And I had a very bad reaction for that from that because I was so sick and was very bad with me. And I we, it was the worst nightmare to be in that in that train. And now when I watch that pictures, what what I saw, I just, this is me what I see what's going, this I was there. That was coming in Auschwitz. We didn't know we going to Auschwitz. There was a big sign: Arbeit macht das Leben süß. [INTERVIEWER] When you came in to Auschwitz and they opened the side of the cattle car, do you remember what the first thing was that you saw? [INTERVIEWEE] We saw the SS men going like this with the and just we didn't we didn't know where we are. We didn't know what, we only saw Germans and a big sign. That's all what I remember. [INTERVIEWER] And what were how were they getting you off of the train? [INTERVIEWEE] Just chasing us like little like animals. [INTERVIEWER] And what did they do once you were off the train? [INTERVIEWEE] Uh, they put us out and they make a selection and they and who went on the right and one went on the left and we didn't know. They put me here, I went here. So uh, [INTERVIEWER] And what about your cousin? [INTERVIEWEE] No, my uh, my uncle. [INTERVIEWER] Your uncle, rather. I'm sorry. [INTERVIEWEE] My uncle uh, I we were separated. Men separated, women separated. and I think that he went and I think that he went on the good side. I never saw him again. And and then they put me on the good side. and uh, [INTERVIEWER] What happened next? Once they separated you, what then happened? [INTERVIEWEE] Then they, then they put you, then they then they told, we followed everything what they say. It was orders, you cannot move because you were staying in line with hundreds of people coming in with these trains and nobody, the the children were screaming and the parents on this side and older people went on the other side and we were staying here on this side. And then they took us into inside to the to the to to Birkenau to to Auschwitz. And they took us in. We didn't know where we were, we didn't know what to do. And they said the they let us go to the to the toilet. And there was no toilet. I remember it like now, I had still some jewelry and some my parents gave me first alone some some gold pieces. I said, maybe I'm going to save myself, maybe you're going to you don't know where you're going to go to work. So I I went in that, when I saw that place, I said no. [INTERVIEWER] Describe to me, please. [INTERVIEWEE] It was so bad. So I went into that to these toilets, they were only wood. And I took off that jewelry and that piece of gold what I had and I throw it in that in the toilet. I said, "I'm not going to give it to you Germans yet too." and then we walked out. They took off our shoes, they took off our clothes, they and they just we walked in, they gave us the showers, they shaved our heads. And I was looking not like a human being. I looked just like a worse than a young man. Just very bad. [INTERVIEWER] What were your feelings and your thoughts when they were taking your clothes from you and doing these things to you? [INTERVIEWEE] Just, you don't you you're just when it happens to all of us, it wasn't that one will be. You go in, you follow like dogs and you're treated worse than dogs. And you don't know what what what what how what's going what's going to be here with you, where are you going to do next? You didn't really care because you were just not a human being anymore. There was no mirror to look just how you look. There was nothing. So what do you do now? Where are you going now? with one dress? [INTERVIEWER] Once they undressed you and took you to the, shaved your head and took you to the showers, what happened next? [INTERVIEWEE] They put us in line and they brought us into barracks. And the barracks were no blankets, no, like a double barrack, double wood where you had to lay down like 13 people were laying on the bottom and 13 on the top. and they they put you down there. They didn't, they give you some watery soup. very little. [INTERVIEWER] What was on these beds of wood? [INTERVIEWEE] Nothing. There was no straw, no Nothing, nothing, nothing, nothing. [INTERVIEWER] Were there any other people in this barrack when they brought you in? [INTERVIEWEE] We all, there there was there people just in we came in with the whole group from Łódź. This was all, this was like the end of the of the Łódź ghetto that was liquidated when all of the Łódź people what came in from Łódź went in this barracks, in this in this one barrack by the other one. [INTERVIEWER] So they would have come in approximately at the same time you had. [INTERVIEWEE] The same time, yes, yes. [INTERVIEWER] Once you got into the barracks, what happened then? [INTERVIEWEE] They just told us that we we're going to have to get up at 4:00 in the morning or 2:00 in the morning when they feel like it. and it was inside were in the in Auschwitz, we had a lot of women's German soldiers, SS women. And the first night when we came in and they took us out for an appeal. They make us first stand and then they make us knee on the knees. And if you bend over, how long can you sit on your knees? and when you're so hungry and tired and and sick, everybody was just, they hit you over the head. On this and just, you could see from the girls the blood was running and everyone and I was getting it too. And how long can you sit on your knees? [INTERVIEWER] You said you were getting hit too. Where were you hit? [INTERVIEWEE] In the head, in the head, in the sides, they just [INTERVIEWER] And what were they hitting you with? [INTERVIEWEE] They had a stick with a, a whip, a whip, with a whip. and they were going from one to the other who who didn't sit to their orders. [INTERVIEWER] How long did they keep you like this? [INTERVIEWEE] We were there on that appeal for about two hours on your knees. and then we got up and then they they went to the, then we went to the toilets. And over there, we we I met a week later when it passed by a week, I met my mother in the toilet. And my mother almost didn't recognize me and I didn't recognize her. My mother without hair and I without hair. So I, so I told her I'm going to take her into my block. She says, "No, we are counted." My mother was in a block where you didn't have anymore this wooden thing. just they were sitting a few thousand people in one barrack sitting one on the other, not standing up, you couldn't stand, you were sitting in the same position you were sitting. [INTERVIEWER] No beds? [INTERVIEWEE] No, no beds, no nothing, nothing, nothing, nothing. [INTERVIEWER] You said they had taken your clothes away from you when you first came in. What did they give you to wear? [INTERVIEWEE] One dress. And uh, just one, that's all. [INTERVIEWER] And what, what was it like? [INTERVIEWEE] Blue and white striped. [INTERVIEWER] And what about shoes? [INTERVIEWEE] No shoes, they give us wooden shoes and I couldn't wear them. They were rubbing my feet and I couldn't wear them. So when I was, [INTERVIEWER] You said you had met your mother. [INTERVIEWEE] when I met my mother, and when they were calling out around 2:00 in the morning that we should go for coffee, so I always when I saw my mother and know what block she was, I offered myself to go and I picked instead to wear the shoes, I took the shoe and I grabbed, I told them I'm going for the coffee, just I didn't. They would have killed me anyway what they were doing. So I put in the barrel where the coffee was my shoe and I grabbed the, and I grabbed that shoe the first time and brought it over to my mother with the coffee where she was sitting so she'd be able to survive, maybe I'll give her that coffee. And that's what I saw her once. and then I wanted, and then I saw her again the next morning. And then they were saying where we went for that soup. So I saw somebody had a little iron pot or something. So I was saying laying someplace, some little pot. So I was running, I I offered myself to go for the for the food, for that soup. I should carry these heavy things for this barrack. So I went and I got this this little pot and I put it, now I walk very slow because nobody should see me with it. So I was holding it between my legs I should be able to bring it over a little bit to drink for my mother. I'll never forget that. Never, never. Then, [INTERVIEWER] What was your daily routine? [INTERVIEWEE] There was no routine. You were, either you were laying on the bridge, on the top, I was laying on the top, or you were laying or you were going out on appeal or you were going to the toilet. That's the day. [INTERVIEWER] They were not sending you to work. [INTERVIEWEE] No, in Auschwitz where I was, we didn't send us to work. Now I was in Auschwitz 10 days only. So the last morning when I saw my mother in the bathroom, I said goodbye and I just walked out to go to my barrack. They lined us up. So I begged on my knees to that German SS woman, "My mother is in the toilet, tell her that I'm going, tell her I'm going away, and that I would like to write her a note. And please let me write her a note. Give me a piece of paper." She says, "You see that chimney? In that chimney, you will be burned in a half an hour. So what do you want to tell your mother?" I just couldn't believe it. And I was saying, that's my life. I'm going to be in a half an hour gone, why should I, I cannot go back into her. So, meanwhile, they, we were only young girls and they didn't, they didn't take us to that chimney, to that crematorium. So they took us to a train and they took us to Bergen-Belsen. [INTERVIEWER] How long did it take you to get to Bergen-Belsen? [INTERVIEWEE] Long time. A few days it took us to go to Bergen-Belsen. [INTERVIEWER] And they put you on the same type of train that you had... [INTERVIEWEE] The same type of trains. [INTERVIEWER] Describe what that was like to me, please. [INTERVIEWEE] Very, very bad. Just, I think that they give us a bread to eat when we went out from that camp. They give us a bread with some water. So we tried to, we didn't know how long it will last that trip. And, [INTERVIEWER] How long did it last? [INTERVIEWEE] I don't remember. It is such a long, and it's such a long time and it was such painful things that one can't even describe you how bad it was. [INTERVIEWER] What do you remember of it? Can you tell me? [INTERVIEWEE] It was just to describe you to lay in that train and then to go to and then to leave your mother. It was the painfullest thing in my life. And uh, I went to, we arrived in Bergen-Belsen, it was just as bad as in Auschwitz. [INTERVIEWER] When you got off of the train, what was happening to people on this train ride? [INTERVIEWEE] a lot of them didn't make it. and a lot of them made it. [INTERVIEWER] Did they ever stop along the way at all? [INTERVIEWEE] I really cannot, I cannot recall. I can't remember. I put it out of my head because it was leaving my mother, it was such a big shock in my life that I just didn't care if I live, if I'm die, because I left my mother. [INTERVIEWER] Now, you went with your mother and you saw her in Auschwitz. What had happened to your father at this time? [INTERVIEWEE] I think that my father was, my mother came in with both of my brothers. They took away from her the children. [INTERVIEWER] How long after you got there did your mother arrive? [INTERVIEWEE] Uh, must have been seven days later. [INTERVIEWER] And the boys came with her? [INTERVIEWEE] And one of my brothers, I heard, went on the good side, my older one, what he must have been about 16. [INTERVIEWER] And the younger? [INTERVIEWEE] So the younger one must have gone right away to the crematorium. [INTERVIEWER] And your father? [INTERVIEWEE] My father, I don't know nothing because my father was still when they took my mother with the children, my father was still hiding in an attic. They took them both, they took my mother from the attic out with with the kids. My father was still there. So we don't know nothing about my father because he must have gone with the next transport. So I really don't know. and and we were they didn't live anymore in the house where we lived. [INTERVIEWER] How long were your mother and you in the camp together before they took you? [INTERVIEWEE] I my mother was only, I left my mother in Auschwitz. [INTERVIEWER] I realize that, but how many days were you together in the camp? [INTERVIEWEE] I wasn't in the camp with my mother. Only in Auschwitz I was with my mother. [INTERVIEWER] No, I mean in Auschwitz. [INTERVIEWEE] Three days I only saw her. [INTERVIEWER] Just three days. [INTERVIEWEE] Just, I thank God that my mother lived for six weeks in Auschwitz. And then she really wanted to, she couldn't make it. She, uh, she took, she was 42 years old. She took a blanket, she must have been so sick or she stole that blanket, I don't know, and they beat her up so much. And then when she lost her children, because my cousin was telling me that she begged her that she should go out with her. She says, "No, I want to die." So, they went, there is a there is a place in Auschwitz where they took the men when they were very sick and then they put them in the crematorium and that was, so she was six weeks in Auschwitz, my mother. [INTERVIEWER] Take a moment. Take your time. [INTERVIEWER] After the train ride, when you got off the train at Bergen-Belsen, what did you see? What was the first thing if you can remember that greeted you when you got there? [INTERVIEWEE] You see, they misled us. We had on the on one of part of Bergen-Belsen, there were some people dressed. It must have been some French people or some from Denmark, we don't know till today. They were on the other side dressed, they must have gone to work in Bergen-Belsen. This was like a showcase to show the world that not everybody is dying here for hunger. And this was when we looked through on the other side, we saw these people. We didn't know who these people were. And then they took us in in this barracks, and these barracks were I think the word it's barracks. It was not a house and it was not a a place to live. Only when you go on a picnic, you go to such barracks. You go to a tent. This was a tent. And you were just laying on the floor on that tent one by one. [INTERVIEWER] And the floor was made of what? Was it the ground? [INTERVIEWEE] Of nothing. On the on the ground. And that's the way we were laying. [INTERVIEWER] Did they also separate you when you came into this camp to the right and left? [INTERVIEWEE] No. No. Because it was not a working camp, it was not a crematorium. [INTERVIEWER] So you went as a group to this tent? [INTERVIEWEE] We were lined up and we were walking, we marched in the same way as we came in. And I met a girlfriend who lost everyone too. So we went to Bergen-Belsen together. And we hold to each other because until today, she's my friend. [INTERVIEWER] About how many people did they put in each tent? [INTERVIEWEE] a few hundred people. [INTERVIEWER] What did the what time of day was it when you arrived there? Do you remember? [INTERVIEWEE] It was daytime. [INTERVIEWER] Did they make any attempt to feed you when you got there? [INTERVIEWEE] maybe they give us some water with some soup, I don't remember exactly. [INTERVIEWER] And what happened next in Bergen-Belsen to you? [INTERVIEWEE] They just didn't do, they didn't do nothing with us. They just didn't give us nothing to do. Just like a, [INTERVIEWER] Describe a day for me. [INTERVIEWEE] they just, they just let us lay there. And then they [INTERVIEWER] Was there a roll call in the morning? [INTERVIEWEE] Uh, I don't remember really because it was such a that camp to me, it was just just with no crematorium. Just and [INTERVIEWEE] anytime when you were calling out that they they we're going to work, we didn't know where we going, I was the first one to pick up my hand because [INTERVIEWER] Did you go to work while you were in Bergen-Belsen? [INTERVIEWEE] No, they didn't. Uh, finally after six weeks being there, I was there six weeks, and they called for work. And they could, he said you could take along a close family if you have. So I had my girlfriend but I met in Auschwitz. So I said, she's, nobody had a name or something, they had a number. So go by it. So I said, this is a sister of mine. So she went with me. [INTERVIEWER] Were you given a number when you went into Auschwitz? [INTERVIEWEE] No, that was when when was the last evacuation from the Łódź ghetto, so they didn't have time to make numbers no more. That was um, they were already the crematoriums were so full and it was too busy to get rid of everyone, so they didn't give no numbers anymore. [INTERVIEWER] Describe to me Bergen-Belsen as a camp. What were the facilities like that you were in? [INTERVIEWEE] Very bad. Very bad. [INTERVIEWER] Describe them to me, could you? [INTERVIEWEE] Just Very, you got up, you you got up and they told you to to get up and uh you went out maybe for for appeal for for a little while and then you then you went to the the toilet and that was the life what you lived and that's all. And they give you some some water or some watery soup. [INTERVIEWER] How often did they feed you during the day? [INTERVIEWEE] Once a day. Once a day. [INTERVIEWER] And you were expected to go back to your tent at that point and just remain there? [INTERVIEWEE] Yes. That's all. [INTERVIEWER] Were there guards in your tent? [INTERVIEWEE] No, there was nothing in the in the tent was only somebody maybe would be, there was nothing to do there. There was not there was not a table, there was not a chair, there was nothing, nothing. There was this just laying like animals. [INTERVIEWER] Could you go out of the tent if you wished to? [INTERVIEWEE] Sometimes we were sneaking out like [INTERVIEWER] What did you see when you would sneak out of the tent? [INTERVIEWEE] Just somebody else sneaking out. That's all. [INTERVIEWER] What did people look like that were in this camp? [INTERVIEWEE] We were all, all these people what what we came in, and we were all in the same boat with all just one coat, one dress. That's what we had. Nothing else. We were just in the on the way out from Auschwitz, I think they gave us a pair of shoes. I remember that at least I think we had, I couldn't wear them, but they gave us a pair of shoes to go out, we were supposed to go to work. We were thinking we're going in Bergen-Belsen to work. [INTERVIEWER] About what, what year was this and what time of year did you go to Bergen-Belsen? [INTERVIEWEE] Uh, it was um, August 28, 1944. And you remained in Bergen-Belsen for six weeks? [INTERVIEWER] For six weeks. [INTERVIEWEE] Yeah. [INTERVIEWER] So you pretty much while you were there, from what you're describing, remained isolated, were isolated. [INTERVIEWEE] Isolated. Nothing. [INTERVIEWER] Did anyone in your tent become ill while you were there? [INTERVIEWEE] Yes. [INTERVIEWER] And what happened? [INTERVIEWEE] Nothing. Just laying. Nothing. [INTERVIEWER] They were not given any care whatsoever? [INTERVIEWEE] Nothing. Nothing, nothing. [INTERVIEWER] Were there any deaths in the tent during that six weeks? [INTERVIEWEE] I was laying with most people what were only uh, between 18 and 22 years old. So uh, at them at this barrack where I was in, there were no deaths in that barrack. And really, when you're so occupied with your life, just laying and thinking what's going to be tomorrow and where you going to go to work, and you want to go out, and you want to go to work and maybe he gonna come a transport will take you. We didn't care where we going to go because just this was a this wasn't this wasn't we were run away we couldn't run away. We couldn't go no place. It was not a place to go. [INTERVIEWER] People tend to help each other when it was necessary, in terms of a little extra bread perhaps if someone was very ill or [INTERVIEWEE] There was such a little things what we got. Uh, I remember that uh, my friend what I met in Auschwitz, she was she had very bad uh, her teeth hurt her and she had and she had she was so sick. So one lady next to her wanted to take her bread. She said that piece of bread. So I said, "You're going to take her piece of bread. I'm going to hide it in my bosom to save it when she will feel better." And she will be not and she will make it, and I will give her that piece of bread. And it took about two days, and she felt better, and I gave her that piece of bread to eat. We remained friends for 52 years. We are still friends. She'll never forget. Sure not. After the six weeks, you said you uh, there was a a call for people to go to work? [INTERVIEWEE] They were calling out that from this barrack, that who wants to go to work. So most of these young people, uh, we went to work. We we we didn't know where we going. [INTERVIEWER] What what type of work were you taken to do? [INTERVIEWEE] Uh, we went to, we went to Salzwedel, to a different city. I we were traveling by I can't remember exactly how we went there. I can't remember by train or we went to Salzwedel and in Salzwedel we were still marched out and they told us that we're going to go to work. And in Salzwedel, there was factories from ammunition. And we were working in an ammunition factory where we were a little more human. I mean, we went out to work, uh we have uh some we we got a soup when we came home from work. [INTERVIEWER] Were you fed while you were at the factory? [INTERVIEWEE] No. No. [INTERVIEWER] How were you treated while you were working at the factory? [INTERVIEWEE] The very surprisingly, that the Germans what were watching us, they were sitting down and eating their good food, the sandwich, bologna or anything what they had and the good stuff that we should watch it and we should see how they eat. And we were so hungry, and nobody would ever they go over to tell them they should give us a piece something. Just they were like teasing us. Just uh there were I had the friend what some uh gave her sometimes a piece of bread from the from the Wachmeisters, not from this what [INTERVIEWER] What are the Wachmeisters? [INTERVIEWEE] They were watching us also but just some older people what were not SS, just they were Wehrmacht. No, it's a different it also they were also in green uniforms as soldiers just they were and they were sometimes they would throw them a piece a piece of bread. Just very, very seldom. Just uh when we were there, I was um trying my girlfriend was trying to steal some potatoes and we were trying to survive because we were so run down from Auschwitz and Bergen-Belsen and from these ghettos that if we wouldn't be able to produce in this factory, because we had to produce so much ammunition a day, so I think that this was the best camp. It wasn't because we we we were we were able to get something to eat, to survive. [INTERVIEWER] Why was your friend able to successfully steal the potatoes or was she caught? [INTERVIEWEE] She was caught once. [INTERVIEWER] And what happened to her? [INTERVIEWEE] And it you in in in German it calls a report a report führer. That was and he saw her in in the bath in in the bathroom and he hit her so hard that he came back and he said, "Where is that girl what I just almost killed her?" He said, "Wo ist das Mädchen, was ich totgeschlagen habe?" And she was staying next to me and lined up to go to work and he knows that she was in that block. And I said, "Don't you dare get out." Just stay and make your and she couldn't. And I make her stand up straight. Nobody knew in in that barrack where we lived to go out to go to work and he was going and looking at the faces to see where that girl is. He never found her because I was holding on to her and I was telling her, "Don't you dare step out because he was looking for you only and for nobody else." For six weeks she couldn't move. And I had to go to put in from this the factory where we were working, she couldn't bend to fill up her machines to fill out to give this. Just she survived. [INTERVIEWER] Were you able to cover for her so that they to [INTERVIEWEE] Nobody in my block where I lived knew that this was the girl. [INTERVIEWER] How was she able to do her portion of work if she was unable to [INTERVIEWEE] I was going over there in the same factory we work and I was trying to fill it up for her. Because when you fill it up and then it came out on the other side finished bullets. So the bullets, you had to fill up the bullets for empty, to fill them up. So I was trying to help her. She survived. [INTERVIEWER] Did anybody notice that you were trying to do her work for her? [INTERVIEWEE] Uh, no, I was very sneaky about it. Very sneaky. [INTERVIEWER] And every night you were returned back to the camp. [INTERVIEWEE] When we returned back for that six weeks, we had a straw sack. Is it in English, straw sack? Uh, uh, where you put straw and we were not laying just on the on the wood. We had straw inside in a sack of straw. And so I had to put the straw sack next to me to be able to that I should be she couldn't lay, I was just bones. So she should be able to put the shoulder on that she that she should be able to I shouldn't hurt her so much because we had only one of each for the straw sack, we didn't have blankets. So we had so that she should be able to be a little softer to lay on the straw for six weeks till she recuperated. [INTERVIEWER] How long did you continue to work in this? [INTERVIEWEE] Till the end of the war. [INTERVIEWER] And what happened at the end of the war? What's the date that you recall? [INTERVIEWEE] Uh, we didn't know a day before the war supposed to finish, uh, the SS men had ready oil oil barrels to burn our camp. When we came into this camp, there were, we were 1,500 girls. I think there were 600 Hungarian girls because the camp was inside already and 800, 900 Polish girls. And uh, they were supposed to burn that camp. Just by luck, the the the one what run the camp, the uh, SS man, he made an order not to burn these girls. So by luck, we survived. The oil barrels were staying somebody told us the day before there were Frenchmen working uh, in the in the factories too and they were like communists or other things what they what they took them over to the camps over there. So uh, so they told us that these barrels are staying over there and tonight, you're going to be this girl is going to be on fire. Just by luck. And then we survived. [INTERVIEWER] Did you have any awareness before the day that the war ended that the war was coming to an end? Did you have any feeling that [INTERVIEWEE] No. [INTERVIEWER] things were changing. [INTERVIEWEE] No. Because this SS people what worked with us, they were such believer in that Hitler that they didn't lose the war yet. They they couldn't believe it. And uh, they were fighting till the last minute. [INTERVIEWER] And were you working in the munitions plant until the very last minute? [INTERVIEWEE] Till the last day. [INTERVIEWER] Tell me about that last day. [INTERVIEWEE] Just like any other day. Nobody know nothing. And what happened? And they we came home and all of a sudden, then the next the next day we got up and we heard the English soldiers came in and we we were free, that we don't have to worry anymore. The doors were open. And that German SS man what didn't let burn our our camp, he was staying and we told them not, he should not kill him because we survived. [INTERVIEWER] Do you know his name? [INTERVIEWEE] No. No. [INTERVIEWER] Describe the day of liberation to me. [INTERVIEWEE] It is a day what we never thought that we'll make it. And uh, it is we were so happy to see that we that we were alive. And the first thing, we were running out to to to get to get to eat. [INTERVIEWER] What time did the British troops come into camp? [INTERVIEWEE] Must have been about 12:00 in the daytime. [INTERVIEWER] Until then, you had not gone out to work that day. [INTERVIEWEE] No, we didn't go out to work that day. [INTERVIEWER] Were the SS officers still in the camp that morning? [INTERVIEWEE] Yes, yes, they were there till the last minute till the Englishmen were in and they were killing them when they came in. [INTERVIEWER] Did you see the British troops enter the camp? [INTERVIEWEE] Yeah. [INTERVIEWER] Describe it to me, please. [INTERVIEWEE] It was the doors were open and they came in and they said, "Don't worry anymore, don't worry anymore, don't worry." But we don't understand what they were talking about because we didn't understand English. And then this Germans what were trying this SS, the one what was still not picking up their hands, the the SS, some of them were shot because they didn't want to uh, give up. And uh, some of them run away. [INTERVIEWER] You were unable to speak the language. How did you indicate to the soldiers not to kill this one SS officer? [INTERVIEWEE] All, I mean, the one what were inside, the one what the one what what hitting my girlfriend, he was killed and I was very happy to see that he was killed. And then was one more guy what was a very mean guy. And he, he once when we were already in the camp to go to steal a potato or something, he took her in front of us and he cut off all her hair. It was already in Bergen-Belsen later on. And he was hitting her in front of us in Bergen-Belsen. So I was happy that he was gone too. You know, just [INTERVIEWER] How were you able to protect the man that you wanted to protect? [INTERVIEWEE] If he did not uh, you know, somehow, the one that want to run away, they run away anyway. [INTERVIEWER] But the one man that did not set the camp on fire, you wanted to protect him when the [INTERVIEWEE] He was staying in the front of us. He was staying in front of the camp and he came in with the hand up. So he was the one. And everyone, the whole camp came over and told him that they told these soldiers that uh, there were some people what spoke German from the soldiers and there were some spoke Yiddish. So we knew and some so we we knew how to when you they understood what we meant when somebody stays like that and is not afraid, so we know that and we told them and in we told them not to touch him, not to touch him. [INTERVIEWER] What was the first thing that the British troops did when they came into camp? [INTERVIEWEE] They told us that we have to, they give us 24 hours that we could go out and and get some food or get some things, 12 hours or 20, one day. And uh, so most people, I was going only for food, personally. Just a lot of people were going and then I just wanted to change my clothes what was so one dress for seven months. So this is what I was getting rid of. And uh, I I just couldn't believe it that we are free. And then we had to decide in this 24 hours we had to decide to stay there or the Russians are coming that we have to leave. Or we should remain in that camp or we should go to further where the English soldiers are. [INTERVIEWER] Did they attempt, the British soldiers, to give you any food? To give you any medical care? [INTERVIEWEE] I think that they did try to do the best with everything. [INTERVIEWER] What about you're getting cleaned up and changing? [INTERVIEWEE] This we did the minute that we we were free for 24 hours, so we were going out to get something and then we were getting some clothes. [INTERVIEWER] From where? [INTERVIEWEE] From the Germans. I told them to give me their dress and they give me a dress, you know. [INTERVIEWER] How were you able to clean up? [INTERVIEWEE] There were water, we had water in camp. We had water in camp. Not warm water but we were cleaning up. We had to go to work every day, we had to be clean in that camp. We just still with just cold water, you cannot keep yourself clean because you didn't have you didn't have panties, we didn't have this, you were just not a person. Just uh, a day later right after that, we had to make a decision. This was a very big decision to make. [INTERVIEWER] Why was it such a big decision? [INTERVIEWEE] Because we had to run again. We didn't know where we going. So they they took us by buses because we didn't want to remain with the Russians. [INTERVIEWER] Why not? [INTERVIEWEE] Uh, somehow they told us something that uh, they're not going to be responsible when the Russians came in, they were raping the girls and thing, we were all young girls. [INTERVIEWER] The British told you this? [INTERVIEWEE] Yes, they prepared us that we could remain if we want to. Uh, just everyone left. I think one girl left. one girl remained. I never heard from her. She was a she was a little older and she was a a patriot from the Communist Party. So she remained, the only one what he told her, I remember. She remained. [INTERVIEWER] And how did they take you from the camp? [INTERVIEWEE] They took us by by uh, British, the British soldiers has such open trucks. We had nothing, we had no packages. So we went to Braunschweig. And we went to Braunschweig to a Kaserne, that means buildings where the soldier, the German soldiers used to stay. And there were we there were we that was closed. They the British took over that part. And we were more safe. [INTERVIEWER] Before we leave Bergen-Belsen, when the British came in, did you notice any reaction on their part to what they were seeing in the camp? [INTERVIEWEE] Oh, they saw everything. They saw everything. [INTERVIEWER] What were some of the things that they saw as they entered the camp? [INTERVIEWEE] This camp where I was, this was not a death camp. This camp I was was a working camp. So they didn't see much. Just in Bergen-Belsen where my husband was, they were saying thousands of peop