e where you where you met, where Jewish people were meeting after the war and was a big city Hanover. And I and he saw me hold when I saw him, I didn't see him, I saw him in the Łódź ghetto. And I hold an egg in my hand and it dropped. And I he asked me, "Why you have such red eyes?" I said, "That's a question." Because men take it six weeks after the war already, you know, it's uh, it's um, and he just he said, "You go over here and I and I will be there in Bergen-Belsen in tomorrow." And I went to Bergen-Belsen and he gave me a room there where he was staying and [INTERVIEWER] What had they set up in Bergen-Belsen? What was it like? And who had organized it? [INTERVIEWEE] It must have been all the English, the British were taking it over and they were making their barracks and they were also in the they also went in in the same places where the soldiers used to be later on. And they lived in this and uh, they got I don't I don't remember it was not like iron beds where the soldiers used to lay. And for humane, I mean, nothing to just like after the war, for us it was heaven. [INTERVIEWER] And what kind of a research center had they set up in order for you to look for people? [INTERVIEWEE] It was a research center made up, there were all, it was also new. All tables and papers and papers and everybody was writing down their names. And there was no computers at that time. There were just people were taking their names and were sitting and and typing and typing. And to to calm yourself down, you were trying to to hope that maybe somebody's alive. And that what what kept you going that maybe just I wasn't lucky. Nobody survived. I was the only survivor. That's That's my life. [INTERVIEWER] What happened once you discovered that you were the only survivor of your family? What did you decision did you make then? [INTERVIEWEE] I was that cousin of mine was very good to me. He gave me clothes, he gave me, he gave me, I could stay with him if I want to. Just I had a friend